 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** Decoding is $O(K)$ for assembling the $K$ Gaussian parameters, assuming constant-time lookup of anchors and exponents. Calibration is $O(V \cdot |P| \cdot |Q| \cdot H)$, where $V$ is the number of views, $|P|$ and $|Q|$ are the sizes of the exponent sets, and $H$ is the number of intervals [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed residual atlas reduces storage by using orthogonal decomposition (rank 10 for state, rank 36 for SH channels) and INT8 quantization [S1, unofficial]. The exact memory savings depend on the number of anchors and the compression ratio of the atlas.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The compressed residual atlas reduces storage by using orthogonal decomposition (rank 10 for state, rank 36 for SH channels) and INT8 quantization [S1, unofficial]. The exact memory savings depend on the number of anchors and the compression ratio of the atlas [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Measured Performance:** The method achieves high-quality continuous-budget rendering, with PSNR results comparable to full models at lower budgets [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables smooth quality-performance trade-offs without retraining; preserves geometric fidelity through residual interpolation; efficient runtime decoding.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-calibration; calibration must be done carefully to avoid overfitting; no per-Gaussian progress correction.
-*   **When to Use:** When continuous budget control is needed for deployment on devices with varying capabilities or when dynamic quality adjustment is required.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When static compression is sufficient, or when per-Gaussian adaptive quality is needed.
